WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.500
Temporary-file handling should be reviewed as a resource lifecycle.

2
00:00:04.600 --> 00:00:14.040
Reviewers should look at creation, directory selection, permissions, data sensitivity, read and write handling, finalization, and cleanup together.

3
00:00:14.140 --> 00:00:20.380
A safe creation call does not compensate for unclear cleanup or a poorly chosen directory.

4
00:00:20.660 --> 00:00:24.420
Tests should cover boundary, failure, and cleanup paths.

5
00:00:24.520 --> 00:00:35.880
Include file open failures, oversized content, partial write handling where practical, validation failure after creation, retry behavior, normal cleanup, and leftover-file checks.

6
00:00:35.980 --> 00:00:39.660
These cases reveal assumptions that happy-path tests miss.

7
00:00:39.940 --> 00:00:42.900
Permission tests can reveal deployment assumptions.

8
00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:50.200
A tool may behave differently under a service account, build agent, container, scheduled task, or developer shell.

9
00:00:50.300 --> 00:00:57.020
Tests and operational checks should confirm that directories and file modes match the intended environment.

10
00:00:57.300 --> 00:01:06.820
Static analysis, compiler warnings, and sanitizers can help find risky patterns around unchecked return values, memory handling, and resource cleanup.

11
00:01:06.920 --> 00:01:11.160
They can also call attention to ignored errors or paths that skip cleanup.

12
00:01:11.260 --> 00:01:15.660
These tools are useful, but they do not replace explicit resource contracts.

13
00:01:15.940 --> 00:01:18.500
Operational checks can also help.

14
00:01:18.600 --> 00:01:27.320
Monitoring for leftover temporary files, unexpected growth, permission drift, or sensitive names in logs can reveal gaps.

15
00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:32.460
Findings should feed back into creation rules, cleanup design, and review checklists.
